\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A zero-radical pairing that is not perfect}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{On the free rank-one #{\mathbb Z}-module #{M=\mathbb Z},
the symmetric bilinear form #{b(x,y)=2xy} separates vectors in
both slots but is not a
\vocabk{perfect pairing}{fdip-001O}. Its currying map is
##{\Phi:\mathbb Z\longrightarrow\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb Z}
  (\mathbb Z,\mathbb Z),\qquad \Phi(y)(x)=2xy.}}

\p{If #{b(x,y)=0} for every integer #{x}, testing against #{x=1}
gives #{2y=0}, hence #{y=0}. Symmetry gives the same conclusion
in the other slot, so both radicals are zero and #{\Phi} is injective.
Every integer-linear functional #{\ell} on #{\mathbb Z} satisfies
#{\ell(x)=x\ell(1)}, so it is determined by its value at #{1}.
But #{\Phi(y)(1)=2y} is even for every #{y}. Thus #{\Phi} misses
the linear functional #{x\mapsto x}, whose value at #{1} is #{1},
and is not surjective. Freeness of #{M} does not repair this failure
of the chosen form.}

\p{The Hom-module convention is
\citet{Section 10.10, Tag 0581}{stacks2017stacks}; the calculation
above proves the failure for this chosen form. For the perfect
counting form and its explicit inverse, see \ref{fdip-001F}.}
